 a new way of thinking about immigrants that was to shape Canadian immigration policy in the latter part of the 20th century. The idea of a mosaic, in which each cultural group retained a distinct identity and still contributed to the nation as a whole, was in contrast to the melting pot, a popular metaphor for the more assimilationist American approach to immigration. The idea of a mosaic of cultures forming a nation was adopted by Canadian sociologist John Porter in his study of social class, entitled Vertical Mosaic: An Analysis of Social Class and Power in Canada. The mosaic theme became a part of Canadian multiculturalism policy in the 1970s, which envisioned Canada as a \"cultural mosaic\".","matchedBy":"infobox_book:high","confidence":"high","attribution":{"label":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canadian_Mosaic","license":"CC BY-SA","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"},"infobox":{"author":"John Murray Gibbon","publisher":"McClelland & Stewart }}","publicationDate":"1938","publicationPlace":"Canada"},"publisherEvidenceId":"publisher-evidence-wikipedia-canadian-mosaic-john-murray-gibbon"},"sources":[{"id":"source-award-record-governor-generals-english-nonfiction-wikipedia-governor-general-s-award-for-english-language-non","label":"Wikipedia: Governor General's Award for English-language non-fiction","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_General%27s_Award_for_English-language_non-fiction","accessedAt":"2026-08-15T17:37:11.749Z","confidence":"secondary","field":"award","note":"Official awards URL: https://ggbooks.ca/past-winners-and-finalists"},{"id":"source-cover-open-library-canadian-mosaic-john-murray-gibbon","label":"Open Library cover for Canadian Mosaic","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL43050336W","accessedAt":"2026-07-15T07:17:13.768Z","confidence":"catalog","field":"book","note":"Cover ID 14920736 recovered from a uniquely matched cached Open Library title/author or ISBN search result; the image is